1
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:05,511
<i>♪ Knowing me, knowing Yule, a-ha</i>

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:07,956
<i>♪ There is nothing we can do</i>

3
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:10,316
<i>♪ Knowing me, knowing Yule ♪</i>

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:13,598
A-ha. What is Christmas?

5
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:17,310
It's a little robin redbreast
petrified by the wind.

6
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:20,791
It's an orphan in a blanket
being helped into a shed.

7
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:27,400
It's a snowman whose nose carrot
has been stolen and eaten by a gypsy thief.

8
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:30,473
But more than that, it's me, Stanley,

9
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:35,032
three stunning hostesses,
some Santa-chat and quality poultry...

10
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:39,034
<i>i.e. "Knowing Me, Knowing Yule
with Alan Partridge".</i>

11
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:42,352
I bid ye good tidings. Now!

12
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:15,269
A-ho-ho-ho!

13
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:37,554
Don't worry. That stuff you saw me
brushing off earlier wasn't dandruff.

14
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:40,872
It was just fake snow.
It's just a polyurethane composite.

15
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:43,998
The type of thing they
used in "Doctor Zhivago".

16
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:47,391
<i>Welcome to "Knowing Me, Knowing Yule
with Alan Partridge".</i>

17
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:50,029
No need to shop around
for festive entertainment.

18
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:52,555
Put your feet up and peruse my chatalogue.

19
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:56,757
And I mean that chatagorically.

20
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:01,714
Tonight's show is coming live
from BBC Television Centre,

21
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:05,953
where we've built an exact replica
of the interior of my house.

22
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:07,753
Whoo!

23
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:12,989
Don't take that as a green light to go
to Norwich and burgle my actual house,

24
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:16,915
as happened
during my television series six times,

25
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:21,119
because sitting at my home
are two men from Securicor, Mike and Ted,

26
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,954
with their two Alsatians,
Tin-Tin and Pickles.

27
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:27,750
So, if anyone's thinking of burgling me,
then don't bother.

28
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:30,070
No, in fact, do. Have a go.

29
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:34,359
Because, quite frankly,
you'll be picking up your teeth with a broken arm.

30
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:39,071
Just think about that
before you trespass on my property...

31
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:41,834
because you people are scum.

32
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:45,478
Let's meet the bell-ringers
of Norwich Cathedral!

33
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:54,238
Knowing me, Alan Partridge,
knowing you, bell-ringers of Norwich Cathedral.

34
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:56,391
- A-ha.
- A-ha.

35
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:59,160
Christmas... a busy time for bell-ringers?

36
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:02,870
Yeah, it is, but it's lovely to
celebrate through bell-ringing

37
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:05,156
the birth of Jesus Christ our saviour.

38
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:08,830
Fair enough.
It's nice to have you on the show.

39
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:10,593
- Thanks. It's nice...
- Great.

40
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:12,791
- Sorry.
- No, carry on.

41
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,598
It's nice to be on a TV show that's not
about swearing and child pornography.

42
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:22,355
Look... Have you seen "Noel's House Party"?

43
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:25,598
- Yes.
- It's that kind of show. Keep it Light.

44
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:27,996
- Have you had a mince pie?
- No.

45
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:30,191
- Have a mince pie.
- I just...

46
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:32,351
Have a mince pie.

47
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:34,909
Next... Next...

48
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:37,390
- All I was saying...
- Mince pies.

49
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:43,713
Later on, Mick Hucknall from Simply Red
will be singing <i>"Ding-Dong Merrily On High".</i>

50
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:47,760
Mick will be backed on a Korg synthesiser
by my musical man

51
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:50,389
with fingers like a centipede,
Glenn Ponder!

52
00:03:55,640 --> 00:03:58,758
Knowing me, Alan Partridge,
knowing Yule, Glenn Ponder. A-ha.

53
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:02,197
- A-ha.
- Well, Glenn. Did you have a nice Christmas?

54
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:05,552
Oh, quiet. We just stayed in, didn't we?

55
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:10,960
- What about you, Alan?
- Very pleasant.

56
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:14,516
Except on Boxing Day,
when I chopped a man's head off!

57
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:17,155
- Really, Alan?
- Yes. I was playing Mortal Kombat

58
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:19,834
on my nephew's video game!

59
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,391
But seriously,
the highlight of my Christmas

60
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:27,349
was receiving a brand-new
Rover Vitesse Fastback,

61
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:31,673
which I don't mind admitting
is the best executive car I've ever had.

62
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:36,318
Wh-What did you get, Glenn?

63
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:41,230
- Andy bought me a silk kimono.
- Gl-Glenn Ponder... and Andy.

64
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:51,398
Now, the idea of this show is that there's a
party atmosphere in my home, my mock-up home

65
00:04:51,560 --> 00:04:56,270
and what party would be complete without
three lovely hostesses hired for the evening?

66
00:04:56,440 --> 00:05:00,991
They'll be circulating
with mulled wine and Boaster biscuits.

67
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:07,509
They're an assortment of chocolate and
hazelnuts compacted onto an oatmeal base.

68
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:09,671
Quite buttery.

69
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:12,394
Anyway, there they are.
Yvette, Martine and Sam.

70
00:05:12,560 --> 00:05:15,074
They're my three Christmas crackers,

71
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:17,914
and I wouldn't mind pulling one of those.

72
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:23,836
Now, while the girls whet our appetite,
who's going to provide us with the meat?

73
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,949
Well, it's a TV chef with a difference,
so let's see what "she" is up to

74
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:34,035
as we go over to my mock-up kitchen
to join daytime TV's favourite chef,

75
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:37,113
the rude Fanny Thomas.

76
00:05:43,160 --> 00:05:46,630
Alan, you've caught me
holding an enormous chopper!

77
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:50,310
- Ooh! Pardon?
- Ha-ha-ha! She's outrageous.

78
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:56,190
- KMAPKYFT. A-ha.
- You what?

79
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:59,313
I'm saying knowing me, Alan Partridge,
knowing you, Fanny Thomas.

80
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:02,558
- What for?
- I'm just trying to save time.

81
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:08,278
We worked out during my series that we wasted
two minutes every show saying my catchphrase.

82
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:11,159
- Why don't you cut your catchphrase?
- Can't do that.

83
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:13,960
That'd be like JFK not saying,
<i>"Ich bin ein Berliner."</i>

84
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:18,875
- He only said it once.
- Yeah, and look what happened to him.

85
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:24,109
Are you saying if you don't say your catchphrase,
you might be assassinated?

86
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:29,918
No... Although I can never completely
eliminate the threat of assassination.

87
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:32,794
I live daily under the shadow of a gunman.

88
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:37,352
But saying the phrase may cause the assailant
to abort the mission in the confusion.

89
00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:40,080
Anyway, I was trying to save time.

90
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:43,557
I could have been shot
four or five times by now.

91
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:49,432
I'm going to have to drop one of the guests.
Cut Zola Budd. Pay her off. Get her a cab.

92
00:06:50,120 --> 00:06:54,717
- Shall I show you what I'm cooking?
- No, there isn't time. We'll see you later.

93
00:06:54,920 --> 00:06:57,958
Basically, we all know
what Fanny Thomas does.

94
00:06:58,120 --> 00:07:02,239
She cooks lovely food and makes innuendos.
Fanny, what are you cooking?

95
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:04,710
Roast partridge. Do you want stuffing?

96
00:07:04,920 --> 00:07:07,958
- Yes, please.
- You don't look the type. Ooh! Pardon?

97
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:10,390
That sort of thing. Anyway...

98
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:12,631
- More of that later.
- You'll be lucky!

99
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:15,076
Outrageous!
Don't talk with your mouth full.

100
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:18,870
- Like my boyfriend last night.
- Please. That's too many.

101
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:24,350
Now on with the party.
My house, my mock-up house,

102
00:07:24,520 --> 00:07:26,750
is open to all comers... Don't!

103
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:30,313
...from the rich man in his Rover

104
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:33,518
to the poor man in his Vauxhall Corsa.
So let's see...

105
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:36,676
Let's see who's here.

106
00:07:36,880 --> 00:07:42,990
Let me just have you for a second.
Now, this man looks like he works in a bank,

107
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:45,276
but, in fact, he's my new boss,

108
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:50,469
the new Chief Commissioning Editor
of BBC Television, Mr. Tony Hayers!

109
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:56,470
I'll have to turf you out. Go over there.

110
00:08:00,720 --> 00:08:04,714
KMAPKYTH. A-ha.

111
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:06,879
Sorry?

112
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:10,554
A. Abbreviation for a-ha.

113
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:12,751
No, I don't want that.

114
00:08:12,920 --> 00:08:15,480
I don't want anyone
to abbreviate the a-has.

115
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:19,833
By the way, when I greet anyone else,
I want the full response.

116
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,276
Only I will use the abbreviated form.

117
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:25,279
This is eating into the
show more than I intended.

118
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:27,636
This was actually meant to save time.

119
00:08:27,800 --> 00:08:30,235
I'll abandon it... No, I'll stick with it.

120
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:36,231
Tony, welcome to the show.
Before you came to the BBC,

121
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:39,358
you were chairman
of Euro-International Airlines,

122
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:42,717
and whilst you were there,
you sacked over 2,000 people.

123
00:08:43,640 --> 00:08:47,713
- Will you do the same at the BBC?
- I don't know about that,

124
00:08:47,880 --> 00:08:52,397
but if I can borrow an image
from your wonderful opening,

125
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:57,834
- I hope to ring some changes.
- My show is your bell.

126
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,514
Please peal it. Peal my bell.

127
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:06,594
- What?
- Sorry. Just tell us what you'll do.

128
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:11,194
Well, I'll be looking at
our output very carefully.

129
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:15,877
We live in a market economy,
and the BBC should be no exception.

130
00:09:16,040 --> 00:09:18,554
Every programme
will have to justify itself.

131
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:21,155
If it's a lame duck, it may go.

132
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:25,672
Well, "Knowing Me, Knowing You
with Alan Partridge" is a very healthy duck

133
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:27,831
with plenty of legs.

134
00:09:28,040 --> 00:09:31,874
Axe any show you like,
just say I'm getting a second series.

135
00:09:32,040 --> 00:09:34,873
Please tell me I've got
a second series, boss!

136
00:09:39,880 --> 00:09:43,714
Do you want to do that?
Just to clear the air.

137
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:47,709
- I know this is not really the time...
- No, it's not. No.

138
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:53,438
No. I'm sorry. I put you on the spot there.
I've embarrassed you. Everyone's embarrassed.

139
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:56,911
- It's gone. It's gone.
- What's gone?

140
00:09:57,920 --> 00:10:02,676
- That moment. The moment's gone.
- Oh, good. I thought you meant the series.

141
00:10:02,880 --> 00:10:05,076
Now, Christmas at home is...

142
00:10:05,280 --> 00:10:08,830
I'm sorry. All that won't affect my chances
of a second series?

143
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:11,951
No, it won't affect the decision.

144
00:10:14,920 --> 00:10:20,199
- The decision's been taken?
- Decisions are made and unmade all the time.

145
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:23,518
So the decision might not have been made?

146
00:10:24,360 --> 00:10:28,274
- The decision is pending.
- So if tonight's show goes well,

147
00:10:28,440 --> 00:10:30,954
I will get a second series?

148
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:34,519
Don't say anything. Don't say a word.

149
00:10:36,440 --> 00:10:39,000
Now, what kind of a Christmas did you have?

150
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:43,751
You know, traditional.
Christmas tree, turkey, all the children together...

151
00:10:43,960 --> 00:10:45,792
- Midnight mass?
- No, I'm Jewish.

152
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:47,798
Are you?

153
00:10:52,120 --> 00:10:55,158
Jews are good. Jews are good.

154
00:10:56,360 --> 00:10:58,829
Knowing you, Tony Hayers, Jew...

155
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:02,600
...knowing me, Alan Partridge, Jew-liker.

156
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:10,070
By the way, all that stuff before,
that's all water under the bridge.

157
00:11:10,280 --> 00:11:13,113
- With the second series?
- No, the Crucifixion.

158
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:17,672
- What...
- No, no, I meant the second series!

159
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:21,430
Let's talk about moral
standards at the BBC.

160
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:24,837
I think many people
are worried about moral standards...

161
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:29,597
Like the young bell-ringer was saying earlier,
people are afraid that the media...

162
00:11:29,760 --> 00:11:34,391
I wasn't talking about the media.
I meant people are worried by sex and violence...

163
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:39,595
Listen, love. We chatted earlier, but you
must remember, you are a peripheral guest.

164
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:43,230
No, no. I think that's a salient point.
It's worth hearing.

165
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:46,592
Good idea. Let's hear her salient point.

166
00:11:46,760 --> 00:11:50,515
Bring her on.
Can we get a drink for the Christian?

167
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:56,316
Sorry, I didn't catch your name.

168
00:11:56,480 --> 00:11:59,233
- Mary.
- Like the Virgin Mary.

169
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:02,358
Are you... It's none of my business.

170
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:07,232
- Yes, I am, actually.
- Oh... Well done.

171
00:12:09,240 --> 00:12:14,872
- Um... Fire away.
- I'd to ask Mr. Hayers why he allows...

172
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:17,031
- How did this happen?
- What?

173
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:19,430
Nothing. Carry on.

174
00:12:19,600 --> 00:12:24,754
Um... I'd like to ask why he allows
on the BBC the graphic depiction

175
00:12:24,960 --> 00:12:28,749
of such sordid practices
as swearing, fighting, masturbation...

176
00:12:28,920 --> 00:12:33,790
Listen, if you're going to talk about this,
can you avoid using that word?

177
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,072
- Masturbation?
- Yes. That's the one.

178
00:12:37,640 --> 00:12:41,156
Well, I do object
to the graphic depiction of onanism.

179
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:43,556
What's onanism?

180
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:47,236
- It's masturbation.
- Thank you.

181
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:53,713
- What's your problem with it?
- I don't like it.

182
00:12:53,920 --> 00:12:55,911
Well, don't do it.

183
00:13:02,200 --> 00:13:06,114
What do you intend
to do about all the filth?

184
00:13:06,320 --> 00:13:08,516
It's not his fault. Leave him alone!

185
00:13:08,720 --> 00:13:12,031
- No, I'd like to deal with this.
- Let him speak!

186
00:13:12,520 --> 00:13:17,117
The BBC has a brief,
part of which is to provide an opportunity

187
00:13:17,280 --> 00:13:20,352
for provocative, innovative,
experimental drama.

188
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:22,551
"Crimewatch UK."

189
00:13:22,960 --> 00:13:25,998
Now, this can't be to everybody's taste.

190
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:28,316
"When The Boat Comes In"
...terrible programme.

191
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:34,999
- But if viewed in context...
- Some programmes are just pornographic.

192
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:41,469
I think to call the BBC's output
pornographic is being slightly hysterical.

193
00:13:41,680 --> 00:13:45,435
Ha-ha-ha! "Slightly hysterical." Very good.

194
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:49,397
I'm not hysterical.
The BBC broadcasts pornography.

195
00:13:49,560 --> 00:13:53,679
Listen, the BBC might be many things,
but porn it ain't.

196
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:56,190
The only way to access
hard-core pornography

197
00:13:56,360 --> 00:13:59,352
is to get a satellite smart-card decoder.

198
00:13:59,520 --> 00:14:01,716
Then you're in business.

199
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:07,472
Look, let's talk about Christmas.

200
00:14:07,920 --> 00:14:10,275
Anyone got any thoughts about Christmas?
Fanny?

201
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:14,110
Well, me spatula's stuck under me flapjack.
Ooh! Pardon?

202
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:16,436
Outrageous.

203
00:14:16,600 --> 00:14:19,319
Hang on. That doesn't make sense.

204
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:23,431
Well, erm... You know, spat... No...

205
00:14:24,760 --> 00:14:26,956
- No, it doesn't work, does it?
- No.

206
00:14:27,120 --> 00:14:31,398
I think it's nice that we're here
to celebrate Christmas.

207
00:14:31,560 --> 00:14:35,872
This is a Christmas show and it's
celebrating the birth of Jesus Christ

208
00:14:36,040 --> 00:14:38,031
and his incarnation as a human being.

209
00:14:38,240 --> 00:14:43,360
No, it's not. It's
"Knowing Me, Knowing Yule with Alan Partridge".

210
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:45,597
- And Christ.
- Christ isn't in the title.

211
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:48,673
It's not "Knowing Me, Knowing You
with Jesus Christ".

212
00:14:50,280 --> 00:14:52,715
- Do you believe in God, Alan?
- Yes.

213
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:58,879
- What is he?
- God is... a... a gas.

214
00:15:02,240 --> 00:15:08,191
- What does that mean?
- Well, not a small... not like Calor Gas.

215
00:15:08,360 --> 00:15:13,639
He's something big like oxygen...
or carbon dioxide.

216
00:15:13,840 --> 00:15:16,514
No, that's bad. That's the devil.

217
00:15:16,720 --> 00:15:22,238
I don't want to get bogged down in this,
because I haven't thought it through.

218
00:15:22,440 --> 00:15:24,477
- Oh, there's someone at the door!
- Is there?

219
00:15:24,640 --> 00:15:27,029
Yes. There's someone at the door.

220
00:15:27,200 --> 00:15:29,271
Santa... Santa...

221
00:15:29,480 --> 00:15:33,758
Let's see who it is.
Ooh! I've got a good feeling about this.

222
00:15:33,920 --> 00:15:36,514
Yes... Oh, it's Father Christmas!

223
00:15:40,360 --> 00:15:42,033
Ho-ho-ho-ho-ho!

224
00:15:42,240 --> 00:15:46,199
- Ho-ho-ho-ho-ho!
- All right, Mike. That's enough.

225
00:15:46,360 --> 00:15:50,433
Father Christmas is, in fact,
none other than Mike Taylor

226
00:15:50,600 --> 00:15:52,637
of the Norfolk Rover Dealer Network.

227
00:15:52,800 --> 00:15:56,191
Mike, thanks very much
for being Father Christmas.

228
00:15:56,400 --> 00:15:58,835
- It's a pleasure.
- Mike, I heard recently

229
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:03,676
that Norfolk Rover are offering 0% finance
on selected models this season.

230
00:16:03,880 --> 00:16:09,512
Yes, the snowy weather has enabled us
to freeze prices on the Rover 600 series.

231
00:16:09,720 --> 00:16:13,076
But, Alan, I understand
you have a Rover Vitesse Fastback.

232
00:16:13,280 --> 00:16:16,033
- Yes, I do.
- What do you like about it?

233
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:20,996
Well, apart from the walnut and leather interior,
which gives it luxury car status,

234
00:16:21,400 --> 00:16:23,960
what impressed me most
was the overall economy.

235
00:16:24,120 --> 00:16:29,399
I mean, 38.4 miles per gallon at 56mph
makes it a class leader.

236
00:16:29,600 --> 00:16:31,511
That's absolutely right.

237
00:16:33,120 --> 00:16:35,839
Father Christmas!

238
00:16:39,920 --> 00:16:42,753
Let's meet a few other
guests at tonight's party.

239
00:16:42,960 --> 00:16:47,397
Over there is WPC Kate Fraser.
She patrols my area.

240
00:16:47,600 --> 00:16:52,674
- I bet she takes down your particulars!
- It's getting quite tiresome now.

241
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,834
And I want you to meet this character.
His name is Alan.

242
00:16:58,040 --> 00:17:02,591
He's responsible for getting me where I am today.
He's my chauffeur!

243
00:17:02,760 --> 00:17:05,832
Yeah, it did deserve one of those.

244
00:17:06,040 --> 00:17:11,240
- Alan, did you get here all right?
- Fine. Bit of a snarl-up at Shepherd's Bush.

245
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:14,313
Lot of Asian blokes
having a party or something.

246
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,992
What is it you call it? Ramadamading-dong.

247
00:17:18,160 --> 00:17:21,152
No, not... It's... It's Ramadan, Alan.

248
00:17:21,360 --> 00:17:25,035
- I know, but you said Ramadamading-dong.
- Not now, Alan. Please.

249
00:17:25,840 --> 00:17:29,674
Now, the conversations
I've just had, I think,

250
00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:33,515
successfully recreate the conversations
I have with my friends at home.

251
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:40,271
Once again, if you've just tuned in,
this is a mock-up. However, this is real.

252
00:17:40,440 --> 00:17:42,670
This is my favourite piece of furniture.

253
00:17:42,840 --> 00:17:45,434
It's a genuine
18th-century Queen Anne wardrobe,

254
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:51,198
hollowed out
to include a 36" TV with companion video.

255
00:17:51,360 --> 00:17:53,476
And I'll just unwrap this...

256
00:17:53,680 --> 00:17:58,231
It's for all those people who asked
what I get up to at Christmas in Norwich.

257
00:17:58,400 --> 00:18:02,678
Here's the answer.
It's "Christmas In Norwich With Alan Partridge".

258
00:18:08,560 --> 00:18:12,519
<i>Norwich. City of mustard... and shoes.</i>

259
00:18:12,760 --> 00:18:14,751
<i>After the bombing of Dresden,</i>

260
00:18:15,080 --> 00:18:20,109
<i>Norwich became the city with the most
pre-Reformation churches in Europe.</i>

261
00:18:20,720 --> 00:18:22,711
<i>I love to jog around Norwich,</i>

262
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:27,795
<i>but I can still make calls to the office
via my digital telecom headset communicator.</i>

263
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:30,636
<i>Norwich Cathedral is breathtaking,</i>

264
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:36,193
<i>and its beautiful stone cloisters are the
perfect place to go jogging every morning,</i>

265
00:18:36,360 --> 00:18:39,034
<i>whilst making
those last-minute budget decisions.</i>

266
00:18:39,200 --> 00:18:42,192
<i>300K. 300K or we take it to Sky.</i>

267
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:46,553
<i>If Raquel Welch doesn't want
to stay in a Trust House Forte,</i>

268
00:18:46,720 --> 00:18:49,189
<i>we'll stick her in a youth hostel.</i>

269
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:54,073
<i>400 years ago, this serenity
was shattered by a bloody big fight</i>

270
00:18:54,240 --> 00:18:56,550
<i>between the townsfolk
and the cathedral monks.</i>

271
00:18:56,720 --> 00:19:01,157
<i>Six friars were beheaded on this site,
which is now a Dorothy Perkins.</i>

272
00:19:01,320 --> 00:19:04,199
<i>The blood runs deep.</i>

273
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:09,433
<i>Many say the ghosts of that carnage
still haunt the town square.</i>

274
00:19:11,320 --> 00:19:15,757
<i>It's three days before Christmas,
and I drive the Rover Vitesse Fastback</i>

275
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:19,231
<i>to the Royal Norwich Hospital.</i>

276
00:19:20,520 --> 00:19:23,239
<i>You can't park here.
I've got special permission,</i>

277
00:19:23,400 --> 00:19:28,713
<i>because I'm distributing a large box
of second-hand toys to the sick children.</i>

278
00:19:31,800 --> 00:19:34,076
Hello. I'm Alan Partridge...

279
00:19:34,240 --> 00:19:36,550
<i>I love kids. The things they say!</i>

280
00:19:36,760 --> 00:19:42,199
There you go. Have a toy. Second-hand,
but it's in quite good condition.

281
00:19:42,400 --> 00:19:46,598
Basically, shop-soiled, but...
it is still in pretty good condition.

282
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:48,950
There's nothing
technically wrong with that.

283
00:19:49,160 --> 00:19:53,119
Does anyone watch my show, "Knowing Me,
Knowing You With Alan Partridge"? No?

284
00:19:53,320 --> 00:19:56,153
- No?
- No one knows it?

285
00:19:56,360 --> 00:19:59,079
- Never heard of you.
- Never heard of me?

286
00:19:59,240 --> 00:20:01,914
It's very rude to say that.

287
00:20:02,080 --> 00:20:07,871
In the studio, we're building an enormous
mock-up of my house... in a studio.

288
00:20:08,040 --> 00:20:12,716
It's great. The budget's about... 280K.

289
00:20:12,880 --> 00:20:18,273
Um, I don't know if any of you
are familiar with TV budgets, are you?

290
00:20:18,480 --> 00:20:21,393
You looking forward to Christmas?

291
00:20:37,360 --> 00:20:41,069
<i>♪ Jingle bells, jingle bells, jingle all the way ♪</i>

292
00:20:42,160 --> 00:20:47,189
The good thing about the automatic
transmission on these things

293
00:20:47,360 --> 00:20:50,990
is you don't have to do
those tricky hill starts.

294
00:20:52,160 --> 00:20:55,994
Right now I just hit the gas, away we go.

295
00:20:59,520 --> 00:21:03,957
<i>It's been a busy few days.
What better way to unwind than at Tandy's?</i>

296
00:21:04,120 --> 00:21:06,999
<i>I've got a special arrangement
with their Norwich branch,</i>

297
00:21:07,160 --> 00:21:10,915
<i>who let me into their store
after they're closed to the public.</i>

298
00:21:11,080 --> 00:21:15,756
<i>If you're a well-known celebrity,
shopping can be an absolute nightmare,</i>

299
00:21:15,920 --> 00:21:21,677
<i>so it's always nice to mooch around without
fear of being threatened or pickpocketed.</i>

300
00:21:25,760 --> 00:21:28,274
Nice action. Very nice action.

301
00:21:33,800 --> 00:21:36,633
Hmm... That is a very nice action.

302
00:21:43,960 --> 00:21:45,394
Yeah...

303
00:21:45,560 --> 00:21:47,551
It's a quality action.

304
00:21:52,120 --> 00:21:54,953
I've got one, though.

305
00:21:55,520 --> 00:21:59,115
Right... Ah, Traffic Master.

306
00:21:59,320 --> 00:22:02,597
- Can these be dash-mounted?
- Yeah.

307
00:22:02,760 --> 00:22:04,831
Excellent. Excellent.

308
00:22:05,040 --> 00:22:09,273
It's like "Crackerjack", this,
all these presents... Oh, Jesus Christ!

309
00:22:10,760 --> 00:22:14,719
If there's any damage,
report it to the BBC. I'm sorry.

310
00:22:15,720 --> 00:22:22,035
<i>It's at times like these, simply browsing
among electrical goods at Tandy's,</i>

311
00:22:22,200 --> 00:22:24,999
<i>that I know who I truly am.</i>

312
00:22:27,080 --> 00:22:29,515
<i>I'm Alan Partridge.</i>

313
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:36,197
Well, it's Christmas Eve,
all the good deeds are done,

314
00:22:36,360 --> 00:22:40,593
the Rover's in the garage
with the Espace and the Toyota Starlet...

315
00:22:40,760 --> 00:22:44,879
runaround for Denise, when she's home...
parked there safely in the garage.

316
00:22:45,040 --> 00:22:49,318
It's a triple garage. It's quite large.
we'll show you a cutaway of that... now.

317
00:22:51,400 --> 00:22:55,394
I'm going to tuck into a turkey.
You can't come in, it's private,

318
00:22:55,560 --> 00:22:57,551
but I'll see you later in the mock-up.

319
00:22:57,720 --> 00:22:59,870
Good night and merry Christmas.

320
00:23:02,680 --> 00:23:06,116
Seriously, a bit of privacy. Thanks a lot.

321
00:23:20,200 --> 00:23:22,669
Caught me chomping on a mince pie.

322
00:23:23,960 --> 00:23:26,031
Do they taste a bit off?

323
00:23:26,200 --> 00:23:28,874
Bit minty? Sort of fermented.

324
00:23:29,080 --> 00:23:32,391
Everyone, the mince pies are off.
Don't eat the mince pies.

325
00:23:32,600 --> 00:23:35,433
Tony, I hope you'll sort out BBC catering.

326
00:23:35,600 --> 00:23:39,798
I'll make it my top priority.
Although there are other things to address...

327
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:45,200
Sorry. Can I interrupt you?
Mary, can I ask you to eat less noisily?

328
00:23:45,960 --> 00:23:51,911
I'm not being rude, but if I hold my
microphone close to your mouth... now chew.

329
00:23:52,080 --> 00:23:56,870
That's what people hear at home.

330
00:23:57,040 --> 00:24:02,319
I'm not being rude,
it's just that you sound like a pig.

331
00:24:02,520 --> 00:24:08,391
- Sorry, Tony.
- Yes, there are things I'll be looking into.

332
00:24:08,560 --> 00:24:11,996
- For example, product placement.
- What's product placement?

333
00:24:12,200 --> 00:24:16,273
It's when a presenter
abuses his or her position

334
00:24:16,440 --> 00:24:19,558
to advertise a specific product.

335
00:24:20,200 --> 00:24:25,593
I see that as a severe breach of contract
and it'll be punished with instant dismissal.

336
00:24:26,080 --> 00:24:29,550
- Ho-ho-ho! Merry Christmas, Tony!
- Oh, a present.

337
00:24:29,760 --> 00:24:33,549
Don't open that now. Don't open that now.

338
00:24:33,760 --> 00:24:37,071
- You don't have to open that now.
- What is it?

339
00:24:37,840 --> 00:24:39,797
It's a Rover.

340
00:24:39,960 --> 00:24:43,351
- There's a special offer, which lasts...
- Mike. Not now, Mike.

341
00:24:44,800 --> 00:24:47,872
I'm going to have some mulled wine.

342
00:24:48,040 --> 00:24:51,920
- Glenn, do you want some mulled wine?
- No, thanks. I'm fine.

343
00:24:52,080 --> 00:24:54,310
Right. Does your friend want some?

344
00:24:55,680 --> 00:24:57,671
- Do you want some?
- I'm fine,

345
00:24:57,880 --> 00:25:01,714
- No, Andy's fine.
- Well, if he wants some, let me know.

346
00:25:01,880 --> 00:25:04,713
- I'll have some, Alan.
- Get your own.

347
00:25:05,040 --> 00:25:07,156
Ramadamading-dong.

348
00:25:07,480 --> 00:25:11,519
Hello again. How are you?
Looking for the mulled wine.

349
00:25:12,520 --> 00:25:14,750
Ah, Boasters. Mmm...

350
00:25:15,160 --> 00:25:18,676
Quite nice, these.
Have you had a Boaster? Have one yourself.

351
00:25:18,840 --> 00:25:24,552
Quite interesting. Three years ago,
Hobnobs were probably the number one biscuit.

352
00:25:24,760 --> 00:25:30,278
Um... it was digestives, then Hobnobs
came along and blew them into the weeds.

353
00:25:30,480 --> 00:25:36,476
So until recently, in the oatmeal kingdom,
Hobnobs ruled the biscuit roost.

354
00:25:37,760 --> 00:25:40,434
But um... then Boasters came along.

355
00:25:40,600 --> 00:25:46,437
I'd love to have been a fly on the wall at
the Hobnob factory when Boasters arrived.

356
00:25:46,640 --> 00:25:48,995
I'm not implying that there are flies...

357
00:25:49,160 --> 00:25:52,357
I'm not implying that flies
are on the wall at Hobnob factories.

358
00:25:52,520 --> 00:25:56,991
I was talking about hypothetical flies...
but on a real Hobnob wall.

359
00:25:58,520 --> 00:26:01,876
Lovely chat. You're doing very well.
I've been watching you.

360
00:26:05,680 --> 00:26:09,753
Don't forget, Mick Hucknall later on,
but now my star guest...

361
00:26:09,920 --> 00:26:12,116
Apart from Mick Hucknall.
That's contractual.

362
00:26:13,120 --> 00:26:18,479
Now, if you read the "Radio Times",
you may be expecting Raquel Welch.

363
00:26:18,640 --> 00:26:21,792
She cancelled... this afternoon.

364
00:26:21,960 --> 00:26:24,156
I can't say anything beyond that.

365
00:26:24,320 --> 00:26:26,880
Five o'clock, she cancelled.

366
00:26:27,040 --> 00:26:29,634
However, we've got
something better, I think.

367
00:26:29,800 --> 00:26:34,033
We've got two very lovely golfers
who are going to step into Raquel's shoes.

368
00:26:34,200 --> 00:26:35,838
One in each.

369
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:39,391
Their story touched the nation.
She used to be his caddy.

370
00:26:39,560 --> 00:26:41,392
Now he is her caddy.

371
00:26:41,560 --> 00:26:43,392
Why? You'll see for yourself

372
00:26:43,560 --> 00:26:47,349
as I introduce a married pair
of top professional golfing people,

373
00:26:47,520 --> 00:26:49,272
Liz and Gordon Heron!

374
00:26:55,360 --> 00:26:58,591
Watch the Queen Anne cabinet.
Watch Glenn's friend Andy.

375
00:26:59,560 --> 00:27:03,394
There you go. Just want to park him there?

376
00:27:04,760 --> 00:27:09,118
Great. Lovely.
You'll be doing three-point turns next.

377
00:27:10,360 --> 00:27:14,274
KMAPKY, Gordon and Liz Heron. A-ha.

378
00:27:14,480 --> 00:27:16,676
A-ha.

379
00:27:16,880 --> 00:27:19,235
That's a nice laugh.
Sounds like a big bird.

380
00:27:19,440 --> 00:27:22,080
It really does.

381
00:27:22,240 --> 00:27:26,393
Now, as I said, your story is
a Christmas fairy tale, isn't it?

382
00:27:26,560 --> 00:27:29,837
You're Gordon Hansel and Liz Gretel.

383
00:27:30,560 --> 00:27:34,519
Allow me, if you will,
to be Hans Christian Alanpartridgesen.

384
00:27:40,040 --> 00:27:42,475
Once upon a time in November 1993,

385
00:27:42,680 --> 00:27:45,433
a young golfer was on the 18th hole.

386
00:27:45,640 --> 00:27:49,076
He was about to putt his way to victory
in the Colgate Cup.

387
00:27:49,240 --> 00:27:52,119
As his flaxen hair blew in the wind,
it started to rain.

388
00:27:52,320 --> 00:27:57,554
Then disaster! A bolt of lightning
struck him down and he was paralysed.

389
00:27:57,720 --> 00:27:59,916
How did that feel?

390
00:28:01,240 --> 00:28:03,231
Sort of felt a bit like...

391
00:28:09,760 --> 00:28:13,469
Wonderful. Wonderful.
What a lovely sense of humour.

392
00:28:14,480 --> 00:28:16,630
I'll just finish the fairy story.

393
00:28:16,800 --> 00:28:20,236
Gordon the golfer
didn't lie down like a dog

394
00:28:20,400 --> 00:28:23,040
and become a bitter
and twisted hateful lunatic.

395
00:28:23,200 --> 00:28:27,319
He picked himself up, and in the tradition
of many other heroic disabled people...

396
00:28:27,480 --> 00:28:34,796
Nelson, Napoleon, Ironside, Daniel Day-Lewis,
Captain Hook and Dave Allen...

397
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:38,914
he became Liz's golfing coach and caddy
in a special motorised cart.

398
00:28:39,080 --> 00:28:43,039
Two years later, Liz putted her
way to glory in the Colgate Cup,

399
00:28:43,200 --> 00:28:48,513
and last week was named Lady Sports
Personality of the Year by "She" magazine.

400
00:28:48,680 --> 00:28:51,638
The end. It's a fairy story.

401
00:28:51,800 --> 00:28:55,350
Gordon, you were robbed
of the use of your legs.

402
00:28:55,520 --> 00:29:00,230
In a sense,
you were a victim of a leg thief.

403
00:29:00,440 --> 00:29:03,478
What would you say to that leg thief?

404
00:29:03,680 --> 00:29:07,719
I'd say, "Why didn't you pick on someone
with more legs than they need?"

405
00:29:07,920 --> 00:29:11,151
- A centipede.
- Exactly.

406
00:29:11,320 --> 00:29:13,675
A centipede with 98 legs
can still get about.

407
00:29:13,880 --> 00:29:16,679
What about a millipede?
Millipedes have a million legs.

408
00:29:16,880 --> 00:29:19,349
- A thousand.
- What's got a million legs, then?

409
00:29:19,560 --> 00:29:22,074
A thousand millipedes.

410
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:27,630
It doesn't seem fair,
the way God gives some things a thousand legs.

411
00:29:27,840 --> 00:29:32,755
I can't believe a millipede has the
intelligence to appreciate how lucky it is.

412
00:29:32,960 --> 00:29:35,270
Then God gives people only two legs.

413
00:29:35,440 --> 00:29:39,513
- God's probably got lots of legs himself.
- Except God's a gas.

414
00:29:41,080 --> 00:29:43,594
Probably got gas legs.
That makes sense. Yeah.

415
00:29:45,440 --> 00:29:49,673
Now, Liz... Lovely Liz...
Much though I respect golfers,

416
00:29:49,840 --> 00:29:52,480
I do find the sport incredibly dull.

417
00:29:53,320 --> 00:29:55,311
- Is it?
- Isn't he rude?

418
00:29:55,520 --> 00:29:59,514
Golf's a great sport.
It's one of the world's most traditional sports.

419
00:29:59,680 --> 00:30:05,517
It dates back to Henry VIII.
He was the fat king with the six wives. Lucky bloke!

420
00:30:06,560 --> 00:30:10,110
He used to play croquet
on the green outside Buckingham Palace.

421
00:30:10,280 --> 00:30:15,593
In them days, London was like a big village.
They called it Londinium.

422
00:30:15,760 --> 00:30:21,153
Anyway, so through the centuries,
the sticks became the golf clubs that we know,

423
00:30:21,320 --> 00:30:25,917
and the balls became smaller,
they became the golf balls that we know and love.

424
00:30:26,120 --> 00:30:29,112
It's very traditional and exciting,
and dull it is not.

425
00:30:29,280 --> 00:30:32,398
Mmm... Gordon, do you get out much?

426
00:30:35,960 --> 00:30:38,076
When I can.

427
00:30:38,840 --> 00:30:43,516
- Do you have any hobbies?
- Yeah, I like making maps.

428
00:30:43,680 --> 00:30:46,479
What? Ordnance Survey maps? A-Zs?

429
00:30:46,680 --> 00:30:49,115
No, maps of imaginary places, you know.

430
00:30:49,320 --> 00:30:52,836
- What do you mean?
- I create them, you know,

431
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:56,277
like a mountain or a pond,
maybe a little stream.

432
00:30:56,480 --> 00:31:01,953
- Right. Do you hang them on the wall or...
- I don't draw them. They're in my head.

433
00:31:02,160 --> 00:31:05,039
Right. Right...

434
00:31:05,680 --> 00:31:07,671
Are they any use?

435
00:31:09,160 --> 00:31:10,958
Not really.

436
00:31:11,160 --> 00:31:17,236
Good. Well, I'd love to walk round your head
sometime. I'm sure it's very picturesque.

437
00:31:17,400 --> 00:31:20,199
No, you can't. It's private.

438
00:31:20,400 --> 00:31:23,995
All right.
I wasn't going to book an appointment.

439
00:31:24,200 --> 00:31:26,430
Liz. Liz...

440
00:31:26,600 --> 00:31:29,911
Lovely Liz with your lovely bird laugh.

441
00:31:30,080 --> 00:31:34,119
In the past, before the...

442
00:31:36,200 --> 00:31:39,830
...I believe Gordon was
a bit of a ladies' man.

443
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:43,789
- In fact, he was known as Randy Gordon.
- That's right.

444
00:31:43,960 --> 00:31:48,193
Now, I like to call him Chairman Mao.
I say, "Morning, Chairman Mao."

445
00:31:48,400 --> 00:31:51,074
Is he a communist?

446
00:31:51,520 --> 00:31:55,150
- I'm in a wheelchair.
- Of course. Chairman Mao. Sorry.

447
00:31:55,320 --> 00:32:01,191
Right, well, Gordon... In the past,
before the...

448
00:32:01,360 --> 00:32:06,719
...you... what I mean is,
do you still have an eye for the fairer sex?

449
00:32:06,920 --> 00:32:11,312
Oh, yes. Still chase the ladies,
but I stop when they get to the stairs.

450
00:32:11,520 --> 00:32:14,672
- Yes. A bit like a Dalek.
- Yes.

451
00:32:14,880 --> 00:32:19,716
Can't go up the stairs, I mean.
I'm not comparing you to a Dalek.

452
00:32:19,920 --> 00:32:23,390
You're more like Davros,
the leader of the Daleks...

453
00:32:23,600 --> 00:32:28,595
half-human, half... I'm sorry.
I'm deeply sorry. I just... Sorry.

454
00:32:29,520 --> 00:32:32,876
Um... You're married to Liz,
there's no escaping that...

455
00:32:33,800 --> 00:32:36,713
um... but in the words of Dr. Hook,

456
00:32:36,880 --> 00:32:40,396
"When you're in love with a beautiful woman,
you watch your friends."

457
00:32:40,560 --> 00:32:43,598
- Do you watch your friends?
- Was Dr. Hook a real doctor?

458
00:32:43,800 --> 00:32:47,191
I've no idea. Can we find out?

459
00:32:47,360 --> 00:32:51,957
Can we find out if Dr. Hook was a qualified GP?
They'll let us know.

460
00:32:52,120 --> 00:32:53,918
Is this you, Alan?

461
00:32:54,080 --> 00:33:00,474
Yes. This is a portrait I commissioned
by a local Norwich artist, David Morton.

462
00:33:00,640 --> 00:33:06,079
He's done a few famous people.
He did Sebastian Coe, the Conservative runner.

463
00:33:07,960 --> 00:33:11,999
- I think your mouth looks a little bit big.
- That's intentional.

464
00:33:12,160 --> 00:33:16,552
He takes the facet of a subject which
is most significant, then exaggerates it.

465
00:33:16,760 --> 00:33:21,550
I'm a chatter. I don't know what I'd do
if my mouth was struck by lightning.

466
00:33:21,720 --> 00:33:25,554
I'm sure you'd find
another hole to talk through.

467
00:33:25,800 --> 00:33:28,314
Lovely. Lovely sense of humour.

468
00:33:34,680 --> 00:33:39,595
You should put the brakes on that...
or drop anchor, whatever you do.

469
00:33:40,520 --> 00:33:43,160
It's nice that you've got all these ramps.

470
00:33:43,320 --> 00:33:48,520
Do you like those?
I don't have ramps in my real home,

471
00:33:48,680 --> 00:33:52,150
because, frankly,
I don't know that many disabled people,

472
00:33:52,320 --> 00:33:55,676
but if we became friends,
then I'd happily purchase a set.

473
00:33:56,480 --> 00:34:01,270
- Don't get your chequebook out just yet.
- No, it's the least I could do.

474
00:34:01,480 --> 00:34:07,271
It'd be nice if you chipped in.
I mean, you would be the major user.

475
00:34:07,440 --> 00:34:10,637
I'm sorry. I would fund them exclusively.

476
00:34:10,800 --> 00:34:13,110
If we were friends.

477
00:34:13,560 --> 00:34:17,269
Right. Now... It says here "announcement".
What's that about?

478
00:34:18,040 --> 00:34:22,318
Oh, that's me. I'm pregnant!
Ladies and gentlemen, in five months,

479
00:34:22,480 --> 00:34:25,393
I shall be giving birth
to a little baby Heron.

480
00:34:25,600 --> 00:34:29,753
Not literally. That would be hideous...
but congratulations.

481
00:34:29,960 --> 00:34:34,158
Yeah, she used to caddy me clubs,
now she's caddying me baby.

482
00:34:34,320 --> 00:34:37,153
Congratulations and
best wishes to both of you.

483
00:34:37,320 --> 00:34:41,871
And a special congratulations to you, Gordon.
Well done.

484
00:34:44,600 --> 00:34:48,833
- What do you mean?
- I'm just saying well done... good wishes...

485
00:34:49,040 --> 00:34:50,678
- I can have sex.
- I know...

486
00:34:50,840 --> 00:34:53,514
- I can still shag!
- Please. It's Christmas...

487
00:34:53,720 --> 00:35:00,558
Being in a wheelchair doesn't mean I don't
feel sexual desire. I actually feel more.

488
00:35:00,720 --> 00:35:02,791
- I'm a better lover.
- Please...

489
00:35:03,000 --> 00:35:06,709
- People don't want to hear about lust...
- I don't... Please!

490
00:35:06,920 --> 00:35:08,991
- "Please!"
- Look...

491
00:35:09,200 --> 00:35:13,751
Don't! Listen, I'm on your side!

492
00:35:13,920 --> 00:35:17,436
I had those ramps installed
at three hours' notice.

493
00:35:17,600 --> 00:35:20,513
There's no disabled access
at the Strand Theatre tonight

494
00:35:20,720 --> 00:35:22,950
because we've got the ramps.

495
00:35:24,000 --> 00:35:29,120
If you're in a wheelchair and want to see
"The Buddy Holly Story", forget it.

496
00:35:31,280 --> 00:35:35,990
This lot didn't want you on the show.
They said you'd be a fire hazard.

497
00:35:36,200 --> 00:35:40,194
I've got a fire extinguisher under my chair
in case you go off.

498
00:35:42,400 --> 00:35:45,074
Anyway, thank you for
sharing your fairy story.

499
00:35:45,240 --> 00:35:50,189
Stay around, enjoy some mulled wine.
Gordon and Liz Heron... a fairy story!

500
00:35:58,480 --> 00:36:00,073
Now...

501
00:36:01,920 --> 00:36:04,230
Now, I know what you're saying.

502
00:36:04,400 --> 00:36:08,030
You're saying to yourselves,
"Alan, this show is a success.

503
00:36:08,200 --> 00:36:10,316
"Granted, there were one or two hiccups,

504
00:36:10,480 --> 00:36:15,475
"but they could be ironed out
if this show went to a second series."

505
00:36:15,640 --> 00:36:18,712
I hope you're listening, Tony.
This is the viewers speaking.

506
00:36:18,880 --> 00:36:23,272
"However, Alan," you're saying,
"you are a spiv, a rip-off merchant.

507
00:36:23,440 --> 00:36:26,831
"A Christmas special,
and you haven't even pulled a cracker."

508
00:36:27,000 --> 00:36:31,631
To those people, I say, "Right. Bring on
the biggest Christmas cracker in the world!"

509
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:38,753
The cracker is...

510
00:36:39,440 --> 00:36:41,431
The cracker is 5ft high...

511
00:36:46,400 --> 00:36:50,439
The cracker is 5ft high,
the height of a large cow,

512
00:36:50,600 --> 00:36:56,118
and 23ft long, that's about the length
of a small Chinese restaurant.

513
00:36:56,280 --> 00:37:01,639
Our thanks for the construction go to
Hilary Simpson of White City Pyrotechnics.

514
00:37:05,240 --> 00:37:10,440
Thank you, Hilary. Hilary, of course,
can be a man's name, like Leslie or Vivian.

515
00:37:10,600 --> 00:37:14,719
Thank you for donating your services for free.
This is a charity pull.

516
00:37:15,200 --> 00:37:20,673
Fanny, don't comment.
Inside the cracker is a kidney dialysis machine.

517
00:37:20,880 --> 00:37:26,876
If we break the record, it'll be on its way
to the Royal Children's Hospital in Norwich.

518
00:37:27,040 --> 00:37:30,556
Here to pull the cracker
are the Royal Plymouth Sea Cadets.

519
00:37:38,040 --> 00:37:41,510
Now, we were going to have Zola Budd
blow the whistle.

520
00:37:41,680 --> 00:37:46,550
She can't so that, so, Gordon,
perhaps you'd blow the whistle instead.

521
00:37:46,760 --> 00:37:50,230
In your own time. On your marks, get set...

522
00:37:50,440 --> 00:37:56,436
The force required is the equivalent of a
Land Rover winching up a small sapling.

523
00:37:58,400 --> 00:38:02,712
Oh, right! The cracker's on fire!
Abort the pull! Abort the pull!

524
00:38:02,920 --> 00:38:05,833
Gordon! Where's the fire extinguisher!

525
00:38:06,040 --> 00:38:09,999
- It's under your chair!
- It's under my chair!

526
00:38:12,200 --> 00:38:15,033
Put my face out! Put my face out!

527
00:38:17,360 --> 00:38:19,749
Ladies and gentlemen,

528
00:38:19,960 --> 00:38:21,997
we have had a cracker fire.

529
00:38:22,160 --> 00:38:26,393
I'm being told the split
does not constitute a complete snap,

530
00:38:26,560 --> 00:38:28,676
and we haven't broken the world record.

531
00:38:28,840 --> 00:38:34,995
I suppose the lesson to be learned is don't
get your props from White City Pyrotechnics,

532
00:38:35,200 --> 00:38:40,639
because... I'm happy to say this on record...
White City Pyrotechnics are run by twits.

533
00:38:41,800 --> 00:38:46,351
And there's one over there.
And he's got a girl's name.

534
00:38:46,520 --> 00:38:50,514
- And he makes pyrotechnics like a girl.
- Shall I do the joke, Alan?

535
00:38:50,680 --> 00:38:56,153
- No, don't do the joke, Father Christmas.
- Go on. Give us all a giggle.

536
00:38:56,360 --> 00:38:59,000
What make of car goes "woof-woof"?

537
00:38:59,200 --> 00:39:03,558
- Is it a Rover?
- Mike, please. Mike, Mike, please...

538
00:39:03,760 --> 00:39:05,956
Help me out here, please. Please.

539
00:39:06,160 --> 00:39:10,711
No, it's not a Rover, it's a...
Vauxhall Labrador.

540
00:39:10,920 --> 00:39:14,072
Ha-ha-ha-ha! Oh! Hilarious!

541
00:39:15,840 --> 00:39:18,992
- There's no such car.
- There is. My dad had one.

542
00:39:19,160 --> 00:39:22,516
We had great times in the
Vauxhall Labrador.

543
00:39:22,720 --> 00:39:28,432
Right, can we clear this up?
Meanwhile, let's have some breakfast bar chat.

544
00:39:28,600 --> 00:39:31,672
Um... A new section.
What shall we talk about?

545
00:39:31,880 --> 00:39:36,238
- What happens with that dialysis machine?
- It goes back to the suppliers.

546
00:39:36,440 --> 00:39:40,991
- How much does a dialysis machine cost?
- About £20,000.

547
00:39:41,200 --> 00:39:45,353
- And how much does this programme cost?
- That's confidential information.

548
00:39:45,560 --> 00:39:48,473
- £300,000.
- That's correct. Yeah.

549
00:39:49,640 --> 00:39:53,474
You could have got 14 dialysis machines
instead of making this programme?

550
00:39:54,880 --> 00:39:59,317
I think if you asked the public
whether they'd prefer 14 dialysis machines

551
00:39:59,480 --> 00:40:03,314
or my Christmas special,
the answer would be unanimous, right, Tony?

552
00:40:03,520 --> 00:40:05,636
Yes, I think it would.

553
00:40:06,800 --> 00:40:10,156
Thanks, everyone,
for a very entertaining breakfast bar chat.

554
00:40:10,360 --> 00:40:14,319
Quite a good section, that.
That could be a regular feature...

555
00:40:14,480 --> 00:40:16,596
if we got a second series.

556
00:40:17,640 --> 00:40:21,599
<i>♪ On the first day of Christmas,
my true love gave to me</i>

557
00:40:21,760 --> 00:40:24,991
<i>♪ A partridge in a pear tree</i>

558
00:40:25,160 --> 00:40:29,233
<i>♪ On the second day of Christmas,
my true love gave to me</i>

559
00:40:29,400 --> 00:40:34,395
<i>♪ Two turtle doves
and a partridge in a pear tree, that's me</i>

560
00:40:34,560 --> 00:40:38,349
<i>♪ On the third day of Christmas,
my true love gave to me</i>

561
00:40:38,520 --> 00:40:41,512
<i>- ♪ Three French hens, two turtle doves...</i>
- This should have been moved!

562
00:40:41,680 --> 00:40:45,833
<i>- ♪ Alan Partridge in a pear tree...</i>
- This should have been moved!

563
00:40:46,040 --> 00:40:49,271
<i>♪ On the fourth day of Christmas,
my true love gave to me</i>

564
00:40:49,480 --> 00:40:53,394
<i>- ♪ Four calling birds...</i>
- Just cut the music. We can't do this!

565
00:40:53,560 --> 00:40:58,031
<i>- ♪ Alan Partridge in a pear tree ♪</i>
- Keep flapping. It looks good.

566
00:40:58,680 --> 00:41:02,230
We've got you there.
I'm just getting this turtle dove down.

567
00:41:02,440 --> 00:41:04,829
I'm sorry. This is the French hens...

568
00:41:05,000 --> 00:41:09,710
Are you all right there?
You don't have to flap any more, love. It's OK.

569
00:41:09,880 --> 00:41:13,111
I just want to keep you
off the dialysis machine.

570
00:41:13,280 --> 00:41:15,476
It's got to go back to the suppliers.

571
00:41:17,560 --> 00:41:21,349
This really should have been cleared.
That should have been cleared.

572
00:41:21,520 --> 00:41:24,876
I'm sorry I've had to
abandon my big number.

573
00:41:25,040 --> 00:41:30,240
There's still 28 people backstage in
funny costumes who were going to dance...

574
00:41:30,440 --> 00:41:35,276
But... what the heck?
The show's still going well. I believe that.

575
00:41:35,440 --> 00:41:38,956
Let's go back to Fanny Thomas
and her Christmas partridge!

576
00:41:44,160 --> 00:41:48,916
Well, I've basted the bird now.
What we need to sort out is the trimmings.

577
00:41:49,080 --> 00:41:53,677
I don't know about you,
but there's nothing I love more than a hot stuffing.

578
00:41:53,840 --> 00:42:00,155
Ooh! Pardon? Anyway, I'd like you to help
me out now by grasping this very firmly

579
00:42:00,360 --> 00:42:03,352
and giving the top a good hard tug.

580
00:42:04,320 --> 00:42:07,676
- What does your mother think of you?
- Very proud.

581
00:42:07,880 --> 00:42:10,872
- Go on. Say something about "proud".
- Sorry?

582
00:42:11,080 --> 00:42:15,039
- About standing proud or something.
- I don't get it.

583
00:42:15,240 --> 00:42:18,710
I mean like, you know...
like a man's member.

584
00:42:19,600 --> 00:42:23,753
I don't want to encourage you.
I'm just surprised you missed that one.

585
00:42:23,960 --> 00:42:26,998
I don't know what you mean.
Go on. Twist it.

586
00:42:27,200 --> 00:42:30,795
- It's quite stiff.
- That's what they all say. Ooh! Pardon?

587
00:42:31,000 --> 00:42:34,516
No, leave it alone. Now, Alan, look.
Here's the stuffing, right?

588
00:42:34,680 --> 00:42:37,479
I want you to grab a great big fistful.

589
00:42:37,640 --> 00:42:43,033
Ooh, he's not shy. Now, stick it right up.
Go on. Stick it right up. Go on.

590
00:42:43,240 --> 00:42:45,231
That's it. Further.

591
00:42:47,240 --> 00:42:49,038
Further.

592
00:42:49,960 --> 00:42:53,237
- Right up to the neck.
- Now, that's enough!

593
00:42:53,440 --> 00:42:57,115
It's rude to point, Alan.
I don't mean your finger. Ooh! Pardon?

594
00:42:57,320 --> 00:43:01,154
- I am not aroused!
- Methinks the lady doth protest too much.

595
00:43:01,360 --> 00:43:03,795
- Put a cork in it!
- I tried, but it doesn't help.

596
00:43:04,000 --> 00:43:07,595
- Knock it on the head!
- Ooh, you're into the rough stuff?

597
00:43:07,760 --> 00:43:12,470
- It's enough to make a girl blush.
- But you're not a girl, are you?

598
00:43:12,640 --> 00:43:17,874
You're Peter Willis, a failed disc jockey
who dresses as a woman for cheap laughs.

599
00:43:19,560 --> 00:43:25,397
Right. This is Peter, right? What do you want?
Do you want Peter or do you want Fanny?

600
00:43:25,560 --> 00:43:27,551
Uh... Fanny. I want Fanny.

601
00:43:32,400 --> 00:43:37,474
Now, I think you pulled out too soon.
Go on, pop it back in. That's right.

602
00:43:37,680 --> 00:43:42,038
Oi, Alan. What do you do for an encore?
Shag a robin?

603
00:43:43,960 --> 00:43:45,951
That's enough...

604
00:43:46,120 --> 00:43:49,431
I'm sorry! I'm sorry!
I need help! I'll get help!

605
00:43:49,600 --> 00:43:52,479
I'm sorry.
My wife left me on Christmas Eve.

606
00:43:52,680 --> 00:43:57,151
I spent Christmas Day on my own here.
Well, not here. This is a mock-up.

607
00:43:57,320 --> 00:44:02,793
This is a disgrace. I'll make sure you
never... NEVER... work on television again.

608
00:44:02,960 --> 00:44:08,956
Please don't take my chat away from me.
Is there any way I can change your mind?

609
00:44:09,160 --> 00:44:10,673
No.

610
00:44:14,440 --> 00:44:17,273
Do you want some? Eh? I'm handy!

611
00:44:17,880 --> 00:44:20,076
I'm handy! Do you want some?

612
00:44:20,240 --> 00:44:22,914
- No, thank you!
- What about you, Mrs. Putter?

613
00:44:23,080 --> 00:44:25,469
Do you want some big bird? Eh?

614
00:44:30,920 --> 00:44:33,719
I will never work in broadcasting again!

615
00:44:33,880 --> 00:44:37,589
And on that bombshell,
it's time for me, Alan Partridge,

616
00:44:37,760 --> 00:44:41,469
to say knowing me, Alan Partridge,
knowing you, wherever you are

617
00:44:41,640 --> 00:44:44,598
and whostso...
Is whost a word? I don't know.

618
00:44:45,800 --> 00:44:51,034
And um... I've just been told Dr. Hook
is not a qualified medical doctor.

619
00:44:51,200 --> 00:44:56,434
Happy Christmas and... Oh, here's Mick
Hucknall to sing "Ding-Dong Merrily On High".

620
00:44:56,600 --> 00:44:57,999
A-ha!

621
00:45:07,200 --> 00:45:09,874
<i>♪ Ding-dong merrily on high</i>

622
00:45:10,080 --> 00:45:12,833
<i>♪ In heaven the bells are ringing</i>

623
00:45:13,000 --> 00:45:15,196
<i>♪ Ding-dong verily the sky</i>

624
00:45:15,360 --> 00:45:18,193
<i>♪ Is riven with angels singing</i>

625
00:45:18,360 --> 00:45:26,199
<i>♪ Gloria</i>

626
00:45:26,360 --> 00:45:29,910
<i>♪ Hosanna in excelsis</i>

627
00:45:31,560 --> 00:45:33,676
<i>♪ May you dutifully prime</i>

628
00:45:33,840 --> 00:45:36,958
<i>♪ Your matin chimes, ye ringers</i>

629
00:45:37,120 --> 00:45:39,430
<i>♪ May you beautifully rhyme</i>

630
00:45:39,600 --> 00:45:42,513
<i>♪ Your evetime song, ye singers</i>

631
00:45:42,680 --> 00:45:50,553
<i>♪ Gloria</i>

632
00:45:50,720 --> 00:45:54,156
<i>♪ Hosanna in excelsis</i>

633
00:45:57,160 --> 00:45:59,754
<i>♪ E'en so here below, below</i>

634
00:45:59,920 --> 00:46:02,799
<i>♪ Let steeple bells be swinging</i>

635
00:46:02,960 --> 00:46:05,395
<i>♪ And i-o, i-o, i-o... ♪</i>

